course: dictionary
lesson: 3354
title: Excellent and its many uses
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Excellent means extremely good, of the highest quality. When a teacher writes «Excellent work!» on your story, she means it is very good indeed. To check, swap in the meaning: «Extremely good work!» still makes sense.
explain: Excellent can also be said on its own to show delight. If you open a present and shout «Excellent!», you are very pleased. Here the word shows how you feel, not what a thing is like.
explain: Look at the words around excellent to see which meaning is used. «Your facts about lava were really clear» is useful feedback: it says what was good and why. «That book ate» is Gen Z slang for «it was excellent», so the context helps you understand a word that looks very different.
explain: Excellent often goes in phrases like «in excellent condition» or «an excellent idea». Ask yourself: is this thing extremely good? If yes, excellent fits.
fact: Excellent means extremely good, of the highest quality.
fact: «Excellent!» on its own is said when you are very pleased.
fact: In school, teachers write «Excellent work!» on good work as praise.
fact: In 1920s American slang, «the bee's knees» and «the cat's pyjamas» meant something excellent; they were playful nonsense.
exercise: Ben opens his birthday present and shouts «Excellent!» What does he mean? | He is very cross | He has lost his bag | He is very tired | *He is very pleased
exercise: «Your facts about lava were really clear.» What kind of comment is this? | A warning to be careful | A sign to stop writing | *Useful feedback that says what was good and why | A question about lava
exercise: Which phrase uses excellent to mean extremely good? | The excellent dog ran off | Excellent wait | Excellent moon, look up | *An excellent idea
test: Mum reads «very good» and «excellent» in Jack's report and says, «Well, I'm not unhappy with that.» What does she mean? | *She is quite pleased with his report | She is cross with Jack | She has not read the report | She wants Jack to write it again
test: Which sentence uses excellent as an interjection, not an adjective? | The excellent cake was shared | The excellent dog is sleeping | An excellent bike is on sale | *«Excellent!» cried Sam, when he won the race
exam: In «He is in excellent condition», what does excellent tell you? | It is a shout of surprise | It is old and broken | It is the name of a place | *It is extremely good, of the highest quality
